WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:04.270
<b>[Music]</b>

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:06.272
<b>&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm Dan Fay.</b>

00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:08.208
<b>I'm actually here at the Project</b>

00:00:08.208 --> 00:00:10.010
<b>Catapult booth where we're </b>

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:13.079
<b>actually making available FPGAs </b>

00:00:13.079 --> 00:00:15.715
<b>in a network configuration for </b>

00:00:15.715 --> 00:00:16.816
<b>academic researchers to use.</b>

00:00:16.816 --> 00:00:18.018
<b>So we have a partnership with </b>

00:00:18.018 --> 00:00:19.119
<b>Intel, and we have these </b>

00:00:19.119 --> 00:00:21.321
<b>deployed down at UT Austin at</b>

00:00:21.321 --> 00:00:23.456
<b>the TACC advanced computing </b>

00:00:23.456 --> 00:00:25.859
<b>center and we invite people to</b>

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:26.793
<b>actually test it out and</b>

00:00:26.793 --> 00:00:29.029
<b>accelerate their research.</b>

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:30.163
<b>So one of the things we have is </b>

00:00:30.163 --> 00:00:32.966
<b>the FPGAs, which you can burn </b>

00:00:32.966 --> 00:00:33.566
<b>down to them. </b>

00:00:33.566 --> 00:00:36.002
<b>They run at the line of hardware</b>

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:37.404
<b>speed, but they run really fast,</b>

00:00:37.404 --> 00:00:38.671
<b>take not too much power.  </b>

00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:40.173
<b>And we have these deployed down </b>

00:00:40.173 --> 00:00:42.342
<b>there, connected with these what</b>

00:00:42.342 --> 00:00:44.444
<b>are called our Taurus </b>

00:00:44.444 --> 00:00:44.878
<b>connectors.  </b>

00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:46.379
<b>So they're all directly </b>

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:47.514
<b>connected point to point from </b>

00:00:47.514 --> 00:00:49.115
<b>each of these cards, but it </b>

00:00:49.115 --> 00:00:50.683
<b>allows you to actually have fast</b>

00:00:50.683 --> 00:00:51.718
<b>speed that doesn't have to go </b>

00:00:51.718 --> 00:00:54.220
<b>out to the CPU and execute.</b>

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:55.188
<b>So this is a quick, short, </b>

00:00:55.188 --> 00:00:55.955
<b>little demo. </b>

00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:57.390
<b>We have a receiver that we have </b>

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:58.525
<b>running on this node. </b>

00:00:58.525 --> 00:01:00.260
<b>And this is running down at </b>

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:00.693
<b>TACC.</b>

00:01:00.693 --> 00:01:02.562
<b>So one of the things I wanted to</b>

00:01:02.562 --> 00:01:04.097
<b>do is actually set this node up </b>

00:01:04.097 --> 00:01:05.532
<b>These are both running down at </b>

00:01:05.532 --> 00:01:07.801
<b>UT Austin at the TACC center, </b>

00:01:07.801 --> 00:01:10.603
<b>this node up as a receiver.</b>

00:01:10.603 --> 00:01:12.205
<b>So it will sit on the network, </b>

00:01:12.205 --> 00:01:14.407
<b>one of these cables, waiting for</b>

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:15.108
<b>information.  </b>

00:01:15.108 --> 00:01:18.978
<b>And then I'll set this one up as</b>

00:01:18.978 --> 00:01:20.046
<b>the transmitter.</b>

00:01:20.046 --> 00:01:21.514
<b>And what you're seeing it's </b>

00:01:21.514 --> 00:01:23.650
<b>sending about eight gigs of data</b>

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:25.085
<b>very quickly over this link </b>

00:01:25.085 --> 00:01:27.887
<b>being processed by the FPGA.</b>

00:01:27.887 --> 00:01:29.355
<b>So we can do it in about seven </b>

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:30.957
<b>seconds really quickly.</b>

00:01:30.957 --> 00:01:32.892
<b>So wanted to thank you for </b>

00:01:32.892 --> 00:01:34.961
<b>taking a look at the Catapult </b>

00:01:34.961 --> 00:01:36.863
<b>and hope please come and join </b>

00:01:36.863 --> 00:01:37.430
<b>us.</b>

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:37.897
<b>It's at </b>

00:01:37.897 --> 00:01:43.269
<b>aka.ms/catapult-academic.</b>

